  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nLake effect clouds amid cyclonic flow departing the eastern Great \nLakes are showing some signs of breaking up. Rising heights and a \nsubtle backing of the low-level flow should slowly chip away\nthe cloud deck for at least a couple peeks of sun late this \nafternoon. Decreasing clouds set us up for, in the context of \nthis mild fall, a very cold night. Lows will tumble toward freezing\n(away from the lake) as high pressure settles over Illinois. \nLight wind to start the evening may set the stage for frost, but\nthen surface wind increases prior to daybreak in response to a \nlow pressure streaking across Ontario. We're now past the \nclimatological growing season; therefore, no frost/freeze \nheadlines. \n\nBreezy but dry on Sunday as the aformentioned low passes to our \nnorth. Forecast soundings show strong flow in the low-levels but poor \nmixing to the surface. This keeps wind gusts in check, \ngenerally from the west at 25 to 30 mph. Cool northwest flow \nbehind this dry frontal passage restarts lake effect clouds \nwhich persist into Monday morning. \n\nBroad northwest flow through the Great Lakes offers times of breezy \nconditions for the week ahead, while a building ridge over the \nCentral US results in a slight warming trend through Wednesday \nafternoon. Given limited moisture transport, little to no rain is in \nthe forecast. Our best chance for any rainfall is not until late \nWednesday and Thursday when a fairly deep trough approaches. \nEven so, this may be a glancing blow as low pressure from the \nMississippi Valley favors rain toward the Ohio Valley, shunted \nsouth by an incoming Great Lakes trough. This trough sets up \nlake effect precipitation, primarily west of our area.",
